# HMD agent

An **HMD agent** is an [[agent]] defined as a HyperMarkDown card. The text of
the card is the agent's instructions, the header of the card is its
configuration, and the operations it performs are other cards. The term is
this project's, and names a sub-specification of the general concept: it says
in what form an agent is written down here. Nothing in it is code.

## Description

### The specification

An HMD agent satisfies seven conditions.

1. **It is a card.** A card with an `agent` table in its header is an agent,
   and a card without one is not. The body of the card is what the agent is
   told. This much is the design of the query language's own book.
2. **Its header is its configuration.** The `agent` table names a model by a
   key of the project's configuration, never a vendor, lists the tools the
   agent may call, and bounds the number of turns. An unknown key is refused.
3. **Its operations are cards.** The agent's text contains no account of any
   single operation. An operation is a card that declares itself an instance
   of [[cognitive-operation]], and the agent performs it by reading that card.
   Writing a new operation card extends every HMD agent.
4. **Its tools are read-only functions.** The tools listed on the card are
   ordinary functions of the query language that read the vault. The agent
   has no tool that writes.
5. **Its results are typed and carry support.** Each result states the
   operation, the input as understood, the output, what supports it, how far
   to trust it, and what was not done. This is the obligation of a
   [[typed-transformation]].
6. **It proposes and does not act.** What it generates is inert until someone
   integrates it, and anything executable in it is inert until someone runs
   it. These are the separate steps of [[render-integrate-execute]].
7. **It describes itself.** Its card is written to the agent, about the
   agent. Because the agent is a card and operations take cards as input, the
   agent can be given its own card, and can reflect on it or propose a
   revision of it.

### Where it lives

Agent cards are kept apart from the knowledge they work on. In this project
they are a vault of their own, the top-level folder `agents/`, beside the
knowledge base. The two are separate namespaces.

- A link in an agent card to a concept, and a link here to an agent card, do
  not resolve as links. A wikilink never crosses from one vault into another.
- The connection is made by the program that runs the agent. It reads the
  agent from one vault and the operation cards from the other, and matches
  them by name.
- The project has one configuration, which binds each vault by an id.

The separation has a purpose beyond tidiness. The knowledge base is what
agents read and propose changes to. The agents vault is what they are. An
agent that proposes a change to a concept and an agent that proposes a change
to an agent are doing two different things, and the second is the one that
bears on strange loops.

### As a language construct

In the language the agent is a value of a type, and the specification above
becomes a capability of that type: perform this operation on this input,
returning a typed result. The capability is written once. Operations and
agents are added by writing cards. The design is the future lens
[[perform-operation]].

### What exists and what is design

| Part | State |
|---|---|
| The agent card, with header and instructions | written, in the agents vault |
| The seventeen operation cards | written, in this knowledge base |
| The three read-only tools | run on the current query language |
| A second vault bound to the project by id | runs today |
| Reading a card as an agent, running it, typed results | design |
| A link from one vault into another | design |

### Why it matters for strange loops

An HMD agent is a document that an interpreter reads in order to act, and
that can be handed to that same interpreter as the thing to act on. If a
proposal the agent makes about its own card is accepted, the next run is made
under a description the previous run helped to write.

That is not yet a [[strange-loop]]. A run, an edit and another run are a
[[feedback-loop]]. It becomes a candidate only where the agent's
representation of itself takes part in changing what it represents, and where
that can be shown from the record of runs and revisions. The specification is
written so that the record exists.

## Relations

- It is a kind of [[agent]] {relation: is-a}: the form in which this project writes one down.
- Its operations are the instances of [[cognitive-operation]] {relation: uses} that exist as cards.
- Each of its results is a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: uses}.
- It performs only the first step of [[render-integrate-execute]] {relation: part-of}.
- Its proposals are decided by a person, as [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: uses} describes.
- It may become a [[reflective-agent]] {relation: distinguished-from}, but is one only if its self-description changes it.
- It differs from a [[quine]] {relation: distinguished-from}: a quine reproduces its description, an HMD agent reads its description and can reason about it.
- It is the agent of the [[observer-card-experiment]] {relation: part-of}, with itself as the observed card.
- It bears on the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}.
- It bears on the question [[ai-oriented-language]] {relation: answers}: instructions, operations and knowledge in one inspectable form.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[agent]].** Whatever can perform the cognitive operations in an open
  list. This card adds only the form: written as a card, with operations as
  cards.
- **[[cognitive-operation]].** The catalogue of operations with inputs and
  outputs. For an HMD agent it is not documentation but the set of things the
  agent can be asked to do.
- **[[render-integrate-execute]].** Three steps kept apart so that generated
  text never runs itself. An HMD agent stops after the first.
- **[[reflective-agent]].** An agent that revises itself through a model of
  itself. An HMD agent has the model, its own card, and whether the revising
  happens is a matter of what is done with its proposals.

## Queries

The relations declared on this card, as edges:

```hql
@[[hmd-agent]] | uplinks | map(e => {to: e.target, how: e.data})
```

The cards that point here:

```hql
@[[hmd-agent]] | downlinks | map(e => e.source)
```

The HMD agents that exist, read from the agents vault:

```hql
source("agents").cards | map(c => {agent: c.name, title: c.title})
```

Future lens that applies to this card: [[perform-operation]].

